<%#
  The 500 page.

  This used to render the error object straight into a paragraph with an escaped-output
  tag. A pg error stringifies with its message, and a pg message is made of the schema —
  `relation "scorecardstore" does not exist`, `column player.authemail does not exist`,
  fragments of the failing statement — so the page handed a little more of the database
  to whoever tripped it, one failed request at a time. Escaping was never the answer: it
  escapes for HTML, so the quotes came out as character references while the table name
  sat there in plain sight.

  So the error is not passed to this template at all. `reference` is: a short code the
  visitor can quote, which is also on the Sentry event as a searchable tag. Without it
  this page would be a prettier dead end — the league is run by volunteers, and "I saw
  error 7F2A1B" needs to be one Sentry search away from the stack trace.

  (Careful with EJS comments: the scanner stops at the first close tag, so don't write a
  literal EJS tag in here.)
-%>
<%- include('header.ejs') %>
<%- include('nav.ejs') %>

<h1>Something went wrong</h1>

<p>
  We couldn't load this page. It isn't anything you did &mdash; the fault is at our end,
  and it has been reported automatically.
</p>

<p>
  Please try again in a minute. If it keeps happening,
  <a href="/contact-us">tell us about it</a> and quote this reference:
</p>

<p class="lead">
  Reference <code class="error-reference"><%= typeof reference !== 'undefined' ? reference : '' %></code>
</p>

<p><a href="/">Go to the home page</a></p>

<%- include('footer.ejs') %>


</body>
</html>
